WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.240
Sports has always felt like

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.253
the polar opposite of virtual reality.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.670
One is physical and tactile,

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:11.370
the other is perceptual and intangible,

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.613
one is mainstream, and the
other's still pretty niche.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.900
But more and more, they're
becoming intertwined.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.650
I think it's time for my workout.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.580
You seem like a natural.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.200
Whoa!

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.690
That's a little too realistic.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:28.800
Athletes, fans,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.170
and people just trying
to get in a good workout

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.000
are discovering that the virtual world

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.920
holds new possibilities,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.190
and companies like Meta, the
corporation behind Facebook,

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.230
are hoping they can draw new adopters

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.540
by getting physical virtually.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.520
So we think, just generally speaking,

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.890
there's a huge application for sports

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.890
in the metaverse and in virtual reality.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.730
And I think it's truly gonna
enhance the way that fans,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.610
players, everyone just
experiences sports going forward.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:57.630
And for the world's

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.760
most valuable leagues and teams,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.340
can these new digital
ecosystems allow fans

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.050
to not only consume

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.170
but participate in
their favorite pastimes?

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.020
Or will they not even make
it out of the starting gate?

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.190
That virtual selfie

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.527
we saw of Mark Zuckerberg,
that's a $10 billion selfie.

00:01:13.527 --> 00:01:15.570
That's what $10 billion buys you.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:17.190
That's where the criticism's coming.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.680
So if you wanted to think
of what is the hardest thing

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.480
we could possibly do in the metaverse,

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.913
it's probably in the area of sports today.

00:01:33.165 --> 00:01:34.248
Oh.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.283
Uh oh. Don't tell me I drowned the ball.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.073
I'm as bad in here as I am in real life.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.690
Whoa.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:52.680
This is Jason Rubin,
or at least his avatar.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.650
He's the vice president of
metaverse content at Meta,

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.110
the company formally known as Facebook.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:59.790
He has a long history in games

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.440
from creating "Crash Bandicoot",

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.723
to forming the gaming studio Naughty Dog.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.410
However, that experience hasn't translated

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:08.820
to virtual basketball.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.160
Somebody in the background
is coding furiously

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.350
to make sure I don't miss at this point.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:14.400
This basketball hoop

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.860
is part of Meta's "Horizon Worlds,"

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.960
a sort of digital meetup space

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.370
where you can build your avatar

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.830
and connect with others
in virtual reality,

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:26.280
doing everything from
work meetings, concerts,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.925
or casual conversations
near a digital beach.

00:02:28.925 --> 00:02:31.025
Is this the metaverse?
Are we in that right now?

00:02:31.025 --> 00:02:34.320
Or what would you say this
is right now on the scale?

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.030
We are in the metaverse in the same way

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.070
that if you're on a webpage,
you're in the internet,

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:39.660
but you are not the internet, right?

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.870
It's gonna take a while for us
to build a massive metaverse

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.520
that feels like a metaverse should be

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.416
and you'll never see every corner of it.

00:02:46.416 --> 00:02:47.624
I mean, that's part of the fun of it.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.650
Now, the metaverse is a buzzword

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:55.170
we've heard a lot in the last year.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.130
The metaverse,

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.800
the next frontier of the internet.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.860
Big tech, big brands, big ambitions,

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.420
people and companies are pouring

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:04.770
hundreds of millions of dollars

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.780
into the vision of the metaverse.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.000
That reportedly is a
place where we can all go

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.500
and hang out and do business

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.300
and pretty much live our lives,

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.060
or at least our avatars
can live their lives.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.440
If you go back to the '80s,

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.417
there was a movie called "Tron."

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:22.980
Who's that guy?

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:24.720
That's Tron. He fights for the users.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:25.687
People started to imagine,

00:03:25.687 --> 00:03:28.470
"What would it be like if
I was inside of the arcade

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:31.110
or what if I was inside of the computer?"

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.490
What would happen in that world?

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.830
What kinda opportunities
would get created?

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.830
This is Ben Grossman, the CEO of Magnopus,

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:41.250
who worked in the VFX and
film industry for years

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.230
before starting to work on bridging

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.840
the physical and digital worlds.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:47.490
The internet right now
is like a newspaper.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:48.630
It's words. It's pictures.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.310
You click on the words,
it goes to other things.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.130
You click on the pictures,
they move into videos.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:56.370
So the internet is connected pages.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.440
What we're moving towards now

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:02.190
is more the way of the world
works: connected spaces.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.440
If each of those spaces can exist

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.930
between the physical
layer that we all live in

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:08.520
and a completely virtual layer,

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.740
consumers will be able
to interact with content

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.680
the way that they want to, in
the context that they want to,

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.960
and probably moving towards

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.123
one universal device
that helps them do it.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:20.850
Building out this new form

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:24.270
of navigating our digital
and physical lives

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:26.520
is something the largest
tech companies in the world

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.400
are dedicating vast amounts
of resources towards.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.430
Meta, Apple, Google,
Microsoft continue to make

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.700
long-term investments
in hardware and software

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.660
to bring consumers and companies
into immersive 3D worlds.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.587
I sound like a boomer, even
though I'm not, but I'm like,

00:04:41.587 --> 00:04:44.760
"I'll just hang out in
the real world for now."

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.117
I am a boomer and I'm gonna say,

00:04:46.117 --> 00:04:48.120
"I just wanna hang out in the real world."

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.490
The term metaverse is pretty problematic

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:53.400
because it originates in science fiction

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.100
and it's associated with a
little bit of a dystopian vision

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.870
that not everybody would
think is, you know,

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:00.480
something we should all be
aspiring towards building.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.190
Unfortunately, all the
other terms that people

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:03.930
have come up with haven't
really taken traction,

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:06.690
and then courtesy of Mark
Zuckerberg's decision

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.060
to change the name of Facebook to Meta

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:10.260
now we have metaverse.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.490
Starting today, our company is now Meta.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.740
Now, we have a new North Star

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.753
to help bring the metaverse to life.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.140
Meta has invested
billions into this space,

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.800
including reporting a $10
billion loss last year

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.733
on its virtual and augmented
reality arm Reality Labs.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.110
This move to the metaverse
started back in 2014

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.120
with then Facebook's acquisition

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.543
of VR headset maker Oculus for $2 billion.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.240
So they were really kind of in

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.220
a very vulnerable business position

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.250
with Google and Apple
effectively controlling

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.050
their access to most of their customers.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.767
So Facebook needed to kind of say,

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:53.010
"Well, I need something that's next

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:55.230
that we can control in its entirety."

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:56.490
They would argue that that's in order

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.230
to drive a better experience,

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.030
or in order to create something

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:01.710
that you couldn't have done while working

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:03.690
through the plumbing of
the traditional internet.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:06.690
I think renaming themselves
Meta is probably an indication

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:09.810
that they are tripling down on this idea

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:12.363
that the metaverse is the next big idea.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:17.700
Companies like Google,
Valve, Sony, and Microsoft

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:21.510
have all introduced products
to the VR and AR space.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.723
Even Apple is rumored to be
developing its own device.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:28.110
Meta has been one of the
more visible companies

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:29.013
in this space,

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.250
aiming to create products and content,

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:34.713
not just for gamers, but for everyone.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.830
However, early versions of what

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:40.800
the metaverse inside
those headsets look like

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.840
have not always been met with excitement.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:47.160
There are larger issues here
outside of Mark Zuckerberg

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:51.899
posting a really weird baby-like
selfie from the metaverse

00:06:51.899 --> 00:06:55.140
and his time on the Rogan Podcast.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.600
It really goes down to
whether or not people

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.870
can even figure out what
they want a headset for.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:04.620
If corporations want headsets
to be the next smartphones,

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:06.570
they're going to have
to make virtual reality

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:08.970
more accessible and fun.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:11.880
One way to do that, sports,

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.630
even simple ones, like throwing
a basketball in a hoop.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.060
Next time I do an
interview, rule number one,

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.560
gotta learn how to do the basketball

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:21.060
before you're doing it, right?

00:07:22.179 --> 00:07:23.262
There it is.
Okay.

00:07:23.262 --> 00:07:25.710
There it is!
Oh! There it is.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:27.840
All right, now I can go home and sleep.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:28.740
Yeah.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.530
But that just gave us something to do.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:32.850
Like, we're talking, right?

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.100
This is what you would do
with a friend doing something.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.620
It doesn't have to be
basketball. It could be whatever.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.560
But this is very different
than sitting on VC

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.510
with your hands in your lap talking.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:46.620
Meta's really excited about
sports and fitness inside VR,

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:48.750
inside the metaverse,
for a lot of reasons,

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:50.190
but one of the main reasons is it tends

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:51.600
to be a social activity,

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:55.320
and VR and the metaverse
are social activities.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.450
We're excited in a lot
of aspects of sports.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:59.190
We're excited in people's ability

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:01.410
to see professional sports,

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:03.480
that they might be stuck at home alone,

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:04.770
you can see it with other people,

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:07.470
more excited about people
playing sports together,

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:09.417
whether it's recreations
of real-world sports

00:08:09.417 --> 00:08:10.800
and virtual reality,

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.190
or it's new types of sports
that only exist in virtuality,

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:15.630
only exist in the metaverse.

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:18.000
And there's also aspects like training

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.490
that are starting to work now where people

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.590
are training to become baseball players

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:25.623
inside virtual reality,
inside the metaverse.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:27.980
All right, we're ready?

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:31.413
Okay, watch my bun there.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:33.653
Drill of the goal is to score

00:08:33.653 --> 00:08:35.190
as many touchdowns as you can.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:37.050
Show me how it goes, so
I'ma need you to show.

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:38.020
How many you got?

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:40.440
Yeah, I got the high score.
Um, alright.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.870
So you got your play call.
Tap the break button.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:43.703
Boom.

00:08:43.703 --> 00:08:45.250
Now you got that, you're good. Hit break.

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:47.811
Okay, cool.

00:08:47.811 --> 00:08:50.261
Boom, and then you gotta
find your guy and throw.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:08:59.760
This is "NFL Pro Era,"

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:02.280
a newly released virtual reality game

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:03.300
that puts you in the helmet

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.790
of your favorite NFL quarterback.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:08.580
You can select your team,
pick your favorite play,

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:10.080
and then just like a QB

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:12.420
either hand the ball
off to a running back,

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:14.100
find an open receiver,

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.593
or take off running, but with a joystick.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:20.491
Oh, you got sacked.
Oh, dang.

00:09:20.491 --> 00:09:23.340
You gotta get that ball out.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:24.773
You gotta get the ball out.
Ball two!

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:28.350
The game's available on a couple headsets

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.600
and made by developer StatusPRO,

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:33.990
founded by former NFL wide
receiver Andrew Hawkins

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:37.080
and former Division 1
quarterback Troy Jones.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.450
The pair started with
developing training products

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:42.420
for pro teams that used augmented reality

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.280
so athletes could practice with both

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.980
real and virtual teammates.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:49.880
Now. they wanna bring that
experience to a wider audience.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.560
Football is such a unique
game because it's something

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:57.240
that you can enjoy and
watch and spend time

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:59.070
with your family doing,
but to get on that field

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:00.480
and see it from the player's perspective

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.370
and actually play it,
I do think you'll gain

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:05.190
a deeper appreciation of what goes into

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:06.870
what you see on TVs on Sundays,

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:08.490
and that's something that
obviously as two former players

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:09.900
we're passionate about.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:11.287
We kinda had this vision of

00:10:11.287 --> 00:10:15.210
"Man, this technology was
going to be the future, right,

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:17.340
and how do we find our space in that?"

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:19.260
We're both former athletes,

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:21.540
so we have a specific
way of looking at it,

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:24.360
and we felt like there was
a way for us to kinda create

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.660
an experience that everybody
could participate in

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.723
from athletes, to coaches,
to front offices, to fans.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:33.000
And in my demo,

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.220
the immersion factor was believable.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:37.200
It felt like I was actually taking a snap

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.120
at Acrisure Stadium.

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:40.530
The sounds of players running at me

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:43.620
made me afraid to hold
onto the ball for too long.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.390
It wasn't perfect, however.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:46.860
While fans of teams might recognize

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:49.050
their offensive line by their numbers,

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.330
their faces aren't as realistic,

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:53.430
and occasionally you'll see some players

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:55.530
defy some laws of physics.

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:58.380
But even when things went
wrong, the game still felt fun.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:00.570
You see the guy to the slot right here,

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:01.560
number three, again?

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:02.730
He's gonna break inside in the middle.

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:05.970
Let's try to hit him in
the middle when he breaks.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:06.803
Oh my god.

00:11:06.803 --> 00:11:07.923
Oh, you got the wrong hand?
Uh oh.

00:11:07.923 --> 00:11:09.483
With a left-handed touchdown?

00:11:14.197 --> 00:11:16.140
"NFL Pro Era" is not the first

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:19.890
American football simulator
to be created in a VR headset.

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:21.510
There have been arcade-like games

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:24.360
like "2MD" and "MVP Football,"

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:27.120
as well as what is essentially
immersive advertising

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:29.220
from Gatorade that
features Peyton Manning.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:30.630
Before we get you back out there,

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:32.610
we need to talk about hydration.

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:34.890
But importantly, it's the first to have

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:37.320
the official license with the NFL,

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:40.680
and it's the first NFL
branded video game of any kind

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:43.770
outside of "Madden" to debut since 2004.

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:46.860
I think the NFL, you
know, just to be clear,

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:49.230
they are the most powerful
sports league in the world,

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:51.390
and I think for them
it's about making sure

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:53.430
that if they're going into
any partnership with someone

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:56.640
it brings value to their
fans and to the league.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:59.777
And when you sit, even
people who work for the NFL

00:11:59.777 --> 00:12:02.010
on the 50 yard line of the Ravens stadium,

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:03.510
or the Eagles stadium
and on the Giants stadium

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:05.490
are like, "Wait, I even realized

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.020
that I've never been in this position."

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:09.660
I think when that started to
circulate around the league

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:11.400
and they started to see
how this could potentially

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.020
change their fans' experience,

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:15.300
they immediately saw the value.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:16.860
Traditionally, fans of the NFL

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:19.170
have been able to have
this experience already

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:21.210
through the "Madden" video game,

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:23.910
a yearly release that
sells millions of copies

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:27.153
despite lackluster reviews
over the last five years.

00:12:29.737 --> 00:12:32.730
"Madden," from publisher
EA, has the advantage

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:35.520
of launching on all the
major gaming consoles

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:38.400
as well as for PC and mobile devices.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.130
Sales for StatusPRO,
however, will be limited

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:43.893
to only owners of
virtual reality headsets.

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:51.660
We don't know exactly
if VR is ready per se.

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:52.980
That's not something we can determine,

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:56.130
but what we know is we
can bring an experience

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:59.310
that your fans have never had.

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:02.730
And our goal and our hope is
to be one of the experiences

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:06.240
that continues to contribute
to the upward tick of VR

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:07.653
and make it more ubiquitous.

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:12.510
All right.

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:13.470
Let's roll.

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:15.930
This is 2369 warm up, take one.

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:17.224
Mark.

00:13:17.224 --> 00:13:19.080
Ah! Okay.

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:20.490
Whenever you are ready.

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:21.450
All right.

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.490
Outside of the draw of
major sports leagues,

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:26.100
like the NFL, one area that is bringing

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:29.580
a larger and more diverse
group of users to VR

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:30.840
has been fitness.

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:33.550
This is 236900 bagatali

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:37.290
and your warm up is in New Zealand.

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:39.660
What's up, athlete? I'm Coach Leanne.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:41.370
Welcome to Sweat Symphony.

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:42.480
Shake everything out.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:44.310
At first glance, this might sound like

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:47.040
the start of any instructor-led workout.

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:50.070
But from inside a headset,
it feels more like a mixture

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.927
of a Peloton class and "Guitar Hero."

00:13:52.927 --> 00:13:55.110
"Supernatural" was essentially created

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:58.140
by two people who hated working out

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:00.360
and wanted to find a
way to make working out

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:03.420
something that was actually
fun instead of dreadful.

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:04.950
This is Leanne Pedante.

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:07.800
She's the head of fitness
for "Supernatural,"

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:10.560
a VR app that feels more
like a fitness studio

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:11.970
than a traditional game.

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:14.790
You put on this headset
and you're transported

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:17.310
to a beautiful immersive location,

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:20.490
like a Galapagos Island or
The Great Wall of China,

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:23.220
and you're smashing or boxing to targets

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:26.760
that are flying at you to
music that you know and love

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:28.620
while being coached by a coach like me,

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:30.840
who is guiding you through the experience

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:33.783
and reminding you what an
amazing athlete that you are.

00:14:36.407 --> 00:14:38.160
"Supernatural's" also structured

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:39.810
more like a fitness studio.

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:42.960
You buy a membership
for around $20 a month,

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:45.120
and you can choose from a list of workouts

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:48.840
like boxing, flow, meditate, and stretch,

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:50.793
and new workouts are added every day.

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:55.320
Launching in the spring of
2020, the app gained popularity

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:58.260
as many people were searching
for ways to work out

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:00.300
while under lockdown,

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:03.507
and notably people who didn't
traditionally visit gyms.

00:15:03.507 --> 00:15:05.580
Myself, I've been a "Supernatural" athlete

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:08.010
for just over a year now.

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:11.670
Fitness presents a lot of
barriers for a lot of people.

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:14.430
A lot of people feel left out of fitness

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:16.500
or they feel unwelcome in gyms,

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:21.150
and "Supernatural" has
the most diverse community

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:24.270
of users and members that
I've ever seen in my career.

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:27.720
We have members from their
20s to their 80s and 90s.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:30.540
We have members who
are using this to train

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:32.760
for ambitious sports, and we have members

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:34.590
who have never worked
out before in their life

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:36.723
and are doing it for the very first time.

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:40.050
Growing and expanding that user base,

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:43.320
especially to an older and
more gender diverse audience,

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:45.300
is incredibly important to companies

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.030
hoping to profit in the metaverse.

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:51.660
On October 28th, 2021,
Facebook rebranded as Meta.

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:53.908
The next day, it announced plans to buy

00:15:53.908 --> 00:15:57.633
"Supernatural's" studio within
for a reported $400 million.

00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:03.450
But in July of 2022, the FTC
sued to block that purchase

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:05.430
saying Meta is trying to buy its way

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:07.620
into a monopoly over virtual reality.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:10.260
In a statement, Meta
claimed their purchase

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:12.270
would only benefit the industry

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:14.220
saying attempting to bar Meta

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:17.130
for making acquisitions
at all benefits no one,

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:18.960
especially if the goal is to encourage

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:20.613
innovation and competition.

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:25.920
While many studios are
hard at work to find

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:27.540
what could be the killer app

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:29.790
when it comes to virtual reality,

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:31.890
the other question that remains is if

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:35.670
this new spatial internet
does materialize,

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:38.130
how will companies profit from it?

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:40.800
And that leads to the
bigger existential questions

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:43.800
around the intimate, some say invasive,

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:46.260
role big tech companies like Meta

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:49.500
and products like Facebook
play in our lives.

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:52.800
Right now, there are a
lot of companies that work

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:54.480
under a surveillance capitalism model.

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:56.970
It's not the greatest word,
but it kind of accurately

00:16:56.970 --> 00:17:00.240
describes the fact that the
people who were once consumers

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:03.900
are actually the product
and their data gets sold

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:07.347
to businesses who then
advertise products to them,

00:17:07.347 --> 00:17:09.750
and that's the economic engine.

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:12.270
This advertising business is massive.

00:17:12.270 --> 00:17:16.980
In 2021, Meta brought in
$116 billion in revenue

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:21.330
and $57 billion in profit from online ads.

00:17:21.330 --> 00:17:24.330
In the past, Facebook has
drawn blistering criticism

00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:27.540
from across the private sector
and government regulators

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:28.950
about its collection and role

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:31.593
in spreading disinformation
and hate speech.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:36.570
And devices that would allow
you to access digital worlds

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:39.900
also have the ability to
access more data about you,

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:41.820
your location, your interests,

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:45.810
and down the line, potentially
even your emotions.

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:48.210
So a lot of concern has been put into

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:51.270
can we create a healthy
economic model in a metaverse

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:54.240
that isn't requiring advertising?

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:57.750
And so the reality is since
the metaverse is really

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:00.150
just a mirror of physical society

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:03.000
by digital means and
the spectrum in between,

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:04.470
you can take any way
that you can make money

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:07.437
in the physical world and
you can just do it digitally.

00:18:07.437 --> 00:18:09.870
Some of the biggest sports
leagues in the world

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:11.820
are already testing this theory,

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:14.280
starting with maybe the most obvious

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:16.323
selling tickets to virtual games.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.421
Jaylen Brown took it in.

00:18:20.421 --> 00:18:22.950
That's a three from Al Horford.

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:27.690
The new era of technology
with the metaverse AR, VR

00:18:27.690 --> 00:18:31.410
is really just the latest
chapter in an effort

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:34.110
the NBA has been making for years,

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:37.440
which is how to connect
the game with fans,

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:40.950
and more fans then can fit in the arena.

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:42.240
This is Jen Chun,

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:44.250
head of partnerships for the NBA,

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:47.760
who have been an early
adopter of VR technology.

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:50.220
Back in 2015, the league was capturing

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:52.200
a VR courtside experience,

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:54.540
at a time when 360 cameras were still

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:56.043
held together with tape.

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:00.840
The actual capture technology

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:02.400
of the cameras has improved,

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:05.100
but I think also it's the experiences

00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:08.040
around where you find those games.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:10.920
So for example, this last season,

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:15.150
in addition to being able
to go into "Horizon Worlds"

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:17.910
to actually see a game in VR,

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.910
you could experience what
we built called NBA Lane

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:23.880
which celebrated our 75th anniversary.

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:27.150
So instead of just being
able to watch the game,

00:19:27.150 --> 00:19:30.060
you could go into the
virtual amusement park

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:32.430
and actually yourself participate

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:37.230
in slam dunk competitions,
firing t-shirt cannons,

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:40.380
and walking around in VR with your friend

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:42.153
or other NBA fans.

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:43.920
This last season,

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:46.050
the Brooklyn Nets and
the YES Network released

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:48.150
what they called the netaverse,

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:49.440
a glimpse at what is possible

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:51.450
in the future of viewing sports.

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:53.400
While far from perfect,

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:56.820
you could view the game from
any perspective on the court.

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:59.250
And just like going to
a game in real life,

00:19:59.250 --> 00:20:01.440
proponents of a digital economy believe

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:04.020
you'll be inclined to
purchase merchandise,

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:07.230
maybe a virtual hat or
jersey for your favorite team

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.660
that your digital avatar can wear.

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:12.570
The NBA has also experimented
in the NFT market

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:15.480
with digital trading
cards based on highlights.

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:17.220
But I think what it really comes down to

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:20.820
is the emotional attachment
that fans have to the games,

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:23.220
to particular moments in the games,

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:27.150
and so to give fans the
opportunity to have a sense

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:29.365
of ownership of certain
moments in the games

00:20:29.365 --> 00:20:32.490
is an incredible connection
directly to the fan.

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:34.410
See what we got here.
Door number two.

00:20:34.410 --> 00:20:36.600
It's unclear whether
this emotional connection

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:39.060
to teams or players is enough to create

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:41.850
real digital values in these spaces.

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:44.880
While Top Shot cards
gained popularity early,

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:47.223
their sales have since
declined drastically.

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:51.570
But for the NBA, creating
these digital experiences

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:54.210
in merchandise are more testing grounds

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:56.283
for how future consumers may behave.

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:01.860
Innovation has to have a
lean forward, bold, testing,

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:04.620
take that first step mentality to it.

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:07.800
It's a build it, they will
come kind of attitude,

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:10.170
but I think it's most important for us

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:13.683
to continue to make sure that
fans can connect to the NBA.

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:17.700
After all, leagues like the NBA or NFL

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:20.100
are slow to change,
especially when it comes

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:21.690
to how we watch.

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:23.430
Sports commands some of the largest

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:26.490
media rights deals in the
world, and changing them,

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:30.600
even to popular streaming
sites, has been a slow process.

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:34.200
But for Meta, they have high
ambitions for how future fans

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:35.673
will engage with sports.

00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:40.110
The ideal scenario for me is
that sports gets to a point

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:42.480
where in order to truly experience

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:44.700
the fandom of the league
you're most passionate about,

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:46.860
or the team you're most passionate about,

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:48.423
you have to do it under quest.

00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:53.160
The history of new
technology being rolled out

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:57.360
certainly supports sports
helping drive audiences.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:00.030
We saw it with Fox
becoming a major network

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:02.520
by going out and acquiring NFL rights.

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:05.970
We saw direct TV go
after NFL rights as well

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:09.150
in order to get audiences
to pay for their products.

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:09.983
Football?

00:22:09.983 --> 00:22:12.633
Up to 13 NFL games every
Sunday if you want 'em.

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:17.623
I love you.

00:22:17.623 --> 00:22:18.600
I know, sir.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:21.240
What we're excited to
see with VR in the future

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:23.100
is the ability for this to become a part

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:24.480
of your common behavior,

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:26.820
the best way that you
could watch, express,

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:29.520
and really engage around
a live sports event.

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:31.680
But as Ben Grossmamn explains,

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:32.700
having the metaverse,

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:35.400
whether that's an AR or VR experience,

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:37.920
be the primary way we consume sports

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:41.370
from a technical perspective
is still a long way off.

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:44.040
Sports is fundamentally
one of the few things

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:46.440
left in society that is very dependent

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:48.723
on the power of live.

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:55.410
Latency, the time it takes between

00:22:55.410 --> 00:22:58.560
I did something and I received
input in the metaverse

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:00.930
is extremely critical and very difficult.

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:03.420
In sports, it's the difference
between win and loss,

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:04.920
and in the metaverse
it's one of the hardest

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:05.753
things to achieve.

00:23:05.753 --> 00:23:07.410
We're gonna take things
up a level and talk

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:09.210
about defense and footwork.

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:10.773
Fists in front of your face.

00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:14.040
The metaverse can feel fun at times,

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:16.620
but also frustrating and empty.

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:19.920
The idea of watching something
inside of it with friends

00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:23.250
often still requires a
lot of time and effort,

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:25.740
and there's almost
always some kind of bugs

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:26.973
in the experience.

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:30.480
I think for people who
are trying to tackle

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:33.090
sports specifically in the metaverse,

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:35.340
the rewards that are on the
table are pretty significant.

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:38.100
There's a lot of money in that area,

00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:39.720
and there's a lot of complexity,

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:42.540
and the solutions to those
problems are pretty ubiquitous.

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:43.830
They apply to so many other things.

00:23:43.830 --> 00:23:45.570
If you can solve the problems in sports,

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:46.980
well, then you can work
backwards from that.

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:50.280
You can do movies and
television type content.

00:23:50.280 --> 00:23:52.320
You could also get into education

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:53.520
and a million other things.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:56.130
It is an argument to be made
that you find those things

00:23:56.130 --> 00:23:58.230
that have the biggest
economic opportunity,

00:23:58.230 --> 00:23:59.880
you work at solving those problems,

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:02.640
and then you use those
solutions to kind of go after

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:04.290
the things that have less treasure

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:06.090
at the end of that hunt, so to speak,

00:24:06.090 --> 00:24:08.240
and I think sports is
one of those sectors.

